use and redistribute them, including commercially, as long as you give credit and indicate any changes. Please attribute the factors to Cornerstone EEIO.","position":{"start":{"line":151,"column":1},"end":{"line":151,"column":1}},"key":"pJqf9MeVbw"}],"key":"pR2VJTbNG2"}],"key":"fKNbdGyftX"},{"type":"details","children":[{"type":"summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What does a country’s tier mean for how much I can rely on its factors?","position":{"start":{"line":154,"column":1},"end":{"line":154,"column":1}},"key":"RTaLq7M6tr"}],"key":"XHFk2vQUVM"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":155,"column":1},"end":{"line":155,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Countries are grouped in four tiers by the input-output data available for them. Tier 0 (US) has detailed annual tables, Tier 1 (China, Japan, South Korea, UK) uses detailed national tables, Tier 2 (60 countries) uses coarser multi-regional tables from FIGARO or ICIO, and Tier 3 (83 countries) has no usable table and uses a template built from other countries. Factors for Tier 3 countries carry more uncertainty. See ","position":{"start":{"line":155,"column":1},"end":{"line":155,"column":1}},"key":"v3ccSSlHRa"},{"type":"link","url":"/io/country-tiers","position":{"start":{"line":155,"column":1},"end":{"line":155,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"IO sources by country tier","key":"ozc55QGeY0"}],"urlSource":"io/country-tiers.md","dataUrl":"/io.country-tiers.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key":"TRW789KRib"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","position":{"start":{"line":155,"column":1},"end":{"line":155,"column":1}},"key":"C7vkv8dFa6"},{"type":"link","url":"/reference/methods-by-country","position":{"start":{"line":155,"column":1},"end":{"line":155,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Methods by country","key":"m3APQGKsUF"}],"urlSource":"reference/methods-by-country.md","dataUrl":"/reference.methods-by-country.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key"